---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { councilPages } from "../lib/paths";

const count = councilPages().length;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About UK Councils"
  description="What UK Councils publishes about local authorities, where every figure comes from, and the rules the site keeps."
  path="/about/"
>
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: "About" }]} />
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>About UK Councils</h1>
  </header>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      UK Councils has one page for each local authority, answering the questions people ask about it from
      official published tables. Today it covers council tax for the {count} councils in England that bill
      it. School term dates, election results, contracts and spending join as their data is added, and
      councils in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland follow.
    </p>
    <h2>The rules the site keeps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every figure comes from an official table, and its source and date are printed directly under it.</li>
      <li>A figure the source does not hold is not shown. A missing figure means a missing section, never a zero or an estimate.</li>
      <li>Councils are compared only where every council has the same complete official year, as in the MHCLG council tax tables, and only within the same class of council.</li>
      <li>No person is named anywhere on the site.</li>
      <li>The site states what a table says and the rule that applies. It says nothing about why a council did what it did.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      How each figure is read and calculated is on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page, and every
      table used is listed on the <a href="/sources/">sources</a> page.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
